Canonical hash: build canonical representation sorted by evidenceKey. I'll add method to IdempotencyAdvisor:

```java
public String hashBatch(String operation, String actorId, BatchIntakeRequest request) {
    List<Map<String,Object>> pairs = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < request.items().size(); i++) {
        var item = request.items().get(i);
        BigDecimal measured = request.measuredWeights() != null && i < request.measuredWeights().size()
                ? request.measuredWeights().get(i) : null;
        pairs.add(Map.of("evidenceKey", item.evidenceKey() == null ? "" : item.evidenceKey(),
                "description", item.description() == null ? "" : item.description(),
                "declaredWeight", item.declaredWeight() == null ? "" : item.declaredWeight().stripTrailingZeros().toPlainString(),
                "measuredWeight", measured == null ? "" : measured.stripTrailingZeros().toPlainString()));
    }
    pairs.sort(Comparator.comparing(m -> (String) m.get("evidenceKey")));
    return hash(operation, actorId, request.requestId(), pairs);
}
```

Wait — Map.of doesn't allow null values; I use "" for nulls, fine. But Map.of with 4 entries ok. Sorting by evidenceKey string. Serialization of List<Map> by Jackson — map key order: LinkedHashMap? Map.of returns immutable map with unspecified iteration order! That would make JSON key order nondeterministic → hash unstable. Use LinkedHashMap or TreeMap. I'll build LinkedHashMap explicitly.

Also stripTrailingZeros on BigDecimal like "0.10" → "0.1" — canonical. But careful: new BigDecimal("0.00").stripTrailingZeros() → "0E-2"? toPlainString of that gives "0.00"? Actually BigDecimal("0.00").stripTrailingZeros() yields 0E-2 whose toPlainString is "0.00". Fine for hashing (deterministic).

